# Functional Anatomy and Physiology of Acid-Base Balance

- **Overview of acid-base balance:**
  - <u>Normal pH of arterial plasma</u> - pH kept at 7.36-7.44 (corresponds to H+ 36-44 nmol/L)
  - <u>Physiological functions of H+</u>:
    - Provision of normal PH for normal tissue functions (enzymes work in narrow range of pH)
    - Chemical control of breathing - low pH (high H+ levels) results in increased breathing for respiratory compensation
    - Influence on concentration of other electrolytes - notably K
  - <u>Regulation of acid-base balance</u> - 3 lines of defense:
    - **Chemical buffers** - 1st line of defense
    - **Respiratory compensation** - rapid 2nd line of defense
    - **Renal control of acid-base balance** - last line of defense
- **Chemical buffer system** - HCO3 in relative abundance in ECF (21-28 mmol/L):
  - <u>Molecular mechanisms of HCO3- buffering</u> - buffer pH by reacting with excess H+ under influence of carbonic anhydrase to dissociate into CO2 and H2O (CO2 subsequently exhaled)
  - <u>Henderson-Hasselbalch equation</u> (HHE) - describes control of pH by renal or respiratory compensations: 
  ![](../img/henderson_hasselbalch_equation.png)
    - **Renal compensation for respiratory disturbances** - changes in CO2 levels is compensated by unidirectional change of HCO3- levels by the kidneys
    - **Respiratory compensation for metabolic disturbances** - changes in HCO3- levels is compensated by unidirectional change of CO2 levels through alveolar hyper- or hypoventilation at a limited capacity
- **Respiratory control of acid-base balance** - pH control through control of PCO2 part of HHE due to chemical control in breathing:
  - <u>Respiratory compensation for acidosis</u> - stimulation of brainstem ventilatory drive to reduce PCO2 and increased pH to the maximal extent of the respiratory system
  - <u>Respiratory compensation for alkalosis</u> - inhibition of brainstem ventilatory drive to increase PCO2 and reduction of pH, but **limited capacity as hypoxic ventilatory drives kicks in during hypoventilation**
- **Renal control of acid-base balance** - pH control through control of HCO3 part of HHE and through acidification and alkalination of urine:
  - <u>Proximal convoluted tubule</u> - **reabsorption of some 85% of of filtered HCO3** through secretion of H+ generated by CA activity (does not lead to acidification of urine): 
  ![](../img/pct_control_of_acid_base_balance.png)
    1.  Under CA activity, tubular cell CO2 and H2O converted to HCO3- and H+
    2.  Cellular HCO3- is reabsorbed into blood
    3.  Cellular H+ is secreted by NHE3
    4.  Secreted H+ reacts with filtered HCO3- to give CO2 and H2O, which is reabsorbed back into blood, seemingly resulting in <u>reabsorption of HCO3</u>
    5.  H+ secreted in reabsorbed as part of H2O, hence there is <u>limited acidification of urine</u>
  - <u>Distal convoluted tubules</u> - determines **net acid excretion by the kidney** (approximately removes 1mmol/kg BW/d through acidification of urine):
    - **Excretion of H+** - H+ generated by CA activity is secreted into lumen by <u>H/K ATPase</u>, which to some extent facilitates reabsorption of luminal HCO3- as in PCT, but are <u>trapped by urinary buffers</u> (PO4 and NH3), resulting in **net acidification of urine**
    - **Urinary buffers** - many buffers to trap H+ intraluminally and prevent reaction w/ HCO3 but PO4 and NH3 appears to be most important:
      - <u>PO4</u> - filtered HPO4 combines with secreted H+ to form H2PO4 which is excreted with Na
      - <u>NH3</u> - NH3 generated by tubular cells reacts with secreted H+ to form NH4+ which is excreted with Cl
    - **De novo generation of NH3** - through deamination of glutamine by glutaminase
